
File permissions

Unix allows 3 kinds of file access - read, write, execute - for
3 levels of users - the user who owns the file, members of the
user's "group", and all systems users.

The "chmod" command changes permissions. Only the owner of a file may change
its permissions. Please read the full explanation of chmod in Section 1 of
the manual. The most common need is to make a shell script executable:
This is done by issuing: chmod +x filename.

By the way, the "superuser" (su command) has the same permissions as the
owner of a file.

